{#- The hero photograph as a real element rather than a CSS background.

    As a background it could not be found until main.css had downloaded and
    parsed, which put the largest contentful paint at the end of a request
    chain. `<link rel=preload>` is the usual answer and does not work here:
    the preload scanner runs before the viewport is known, so its media
    attribute is ignored and phones fetched the desktop file too. A <picture>
    source is evaluated properly, so each screen requests one file and the
    browser finds it while still parsing the HTML. -#}
<picture class="hero__bg">
  <source media="(min-width: 44.0625rem)" srcset="/assets/img/hero-wall.jpg" width="1600" height="900">
  <img src="/assets/img/hero-wall-mobile.jpg" alt="" width="820" height="1093" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
</picture>
